    <h2>Big Roofs, Small Downspouts</h2>

    <p>Drive the newer streets off Zierdt Road, Balch Road, and Old Madison Pike and you're looking at two-story homes with 2,400 square feet of roof draining through gutters a builder sized for a starter house. The math is simple and unforgiving: one 2x3 downspout carries the runoff from about 600 square feet of roof; a 3x4 carries roughly 1,200. When a whole roof plane funnels to one corner and there's a single 2x3 waiting for it, that corner overflows in every real rain. The water sheets down the wall, streaks the brick, and lands right where you don't want it — at the base of the foundation.</p>

    <p>The fix on these homes is 6-inch gutter with 3x4 downspouts and, on the long planes, a second outlet so no single spout is doing all the work. It's not exotic and it's not expensive relative to a house in the Bob Jones or James Clemens school zones — it's just not what the builder installed, because the builder was buying the cheapest system that passed a walkthrough.</p>

    <h2>The Real Madison Problem Is Drainage, Not Gutters</h2>

    <p>Here's the thing most homeowners out here get wrong, and it's worth saying plainly: on a 12-year-old Madison house the gutters are often fine. The problem is the downspout dumps 18 inches off the slab onto a lot the developer graded nearly flat to fit more houses per acre. Water leaves the spout and comes straight back to the foundation, because there's nowhere else for it to go. You can replace every gutter on the house and still have a wet crawlspace, because you fixed the wrong thing.</p>

    <p>What works is getting the water away from the house: tie the downspouts into 4-inch solid PVC and run it to daylight at the back of the lot or to a pop-up emitter 8 to 10 feet out. The detail that matters — and the one cheap crews skip — is solid pipe, not the flexible corrugated stuff. Corrugated drain pipe looks the same going in the ground, but the ridges silt up within a couple of years and tree roots invade the corrugations, and then you've buried a clog you can't reach. A good drainage job on a flat Madison lot is often more valuable than new gutters, and a contractor who tells you that instead of upselling a full replacement is the one to hire.</p>

    <h2>HOA Rules and the Finish Nobody Reads</h2>

    <p>Most Madison HOAs require gutters and downspouts to match the fascia or an approved trim color, and that part is easy. The part that comes back to bite people is the finish. Two aluminum gutters can be the same color on install day and look completely different in six years — a baked-on Kynar or PVDF coating holds color past 20 years, while a cheaper polyester finish chalks and fades in 6 to 8. The faded one is exactly what generates the HOA compliance letter. When you're comparing bids that look identical, the finish spec is usually the difference, and it's usually not on the first page of the quote.</p>

    <p>The other builder shortcut worth checking: gutters hung dead-level. On a long, straight modern fascia a level gutter looks perfect, so that's how it gets installed — with zero pitch. A gutter needs about a quarter-inch of fall per 10 feet toward the outlet, or it holds standing water that breeds mosquitoes in July and leaves a dark streak down the face. If your gutters have a waterline stain inside, they're level, and that's a re-hang, not a replacement.</p>

    <h2>Gutters Are Part of a Bigger Exterior</h2>

    <p>Drainage problems rarely travel alone. The same storms that overwhelm an undersized gutter also age the roof, and a homeowner sizing up the gutters is often due for a look from a <a href="/locations/madison/roofer/">Madison roofer</a> at the same time — matching the new gutter into a fresh drip edge is cleaner than retrofitting later. The chalky streaks a faded downspout leaves on brick are what a <a href="/locations/madison/pressure-washing/">Madison pressure washing</a> crew brings back, and if you're refreshing trim to satisfy the HOA, the <a href="/locations/madison/painter/">painter</a> and the gutter color need to be chosen together, not six months apart. The <a href="/locations/madison/">Madison contractor overview</a> walks through how these trades fit together across the city.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Gutters and Drainage Cost in Madison</h2>

    <p>Installed ranges for a typical newer home:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>6-inch seamless aluminum</strong> — about $10 to $12 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Buried downspout drain lines</strong> — about $8 to $15 per linear foot trenched, depending on run length and lot.</li>
      <li><strong>Upgraded Kynar/PVDF finish</strong> — a modest premium over builder polyester; worth it for HOA color longevity.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A newer two-story with roughly 200 feet of run plus proper downspout drainage generally runs $2,800 to $4,500. Gutters alone are the cheap part; the drainage is where the value is on a flat lot.</p>

    <h2>When to Do It</h2>

    <p>If you watched water stand against the foundation this past season, do the drainage before the next wet stretch — foundation and crawlspace damage compounds, and it's far cheaper to move the water than to fix what standing water did. Gutter re-hangs and upsizes are fine year-round in this climate; the drainage trenching goes easier when the ground isn't saturated.</p>

    <h2>Questions Madison Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Why does water pool near my foundation?</strong></p>
    <p>The downspout dumps too close on a flat-graded lot. Extend it underground to daylight or a pop-up emitter 8 to 10 feet out, in solid PVC.</p>

    <p><strong>What size do big rooflines need?</strong></p>
    <p>6-inch gutter, 3x4 downspouts, and a second outlet on long planes. A 2x3 only handles about 600 square feet of roof.</p>

    <p><strong>Does my HOA care about gutter color?</strong></p>
    <p>Yes — match the fascia or an approved color, and get a Kynar/PVDF finish so it doesn't chalk and draw a letter in six years.</p>

    <p><strong>Are standing-water gutters normal?</strong></p>
    <p>No. They were hung level. They need a quarter-inch of fall per 10 feet. It's a re-hang.</p>

